Yes — **this is the view I meant.** You have successfully descended into `I_DECODE`.

What you’re seeing is Design Compiler’s **generic implementation of the `DECODE` RTL**, before technology mapping. The lab specifically says that before compile you will see **GTECH components**, rather than gates from the target technology library. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="0"}

The reason it looks much more complicated than your VHDL is that DC has converted behavioral code such as:

```vhdl
if (Crnt_Instrn(25)) = '1' then
    Neg   := not Neg_Flag;
    Carry := not Carry_Flag;
    Zro   := not Zro_Flag;
...
end if;
```

into actual logic structures such as **inverters, muxes, AND/OR gates and comparison logic**.

For example, this part:

```vhdl
if (Crnt_Instrn(25)) = '1' then
    Neg := not Neg_Flag;
else
    Neg := Neg_Flag;
end if;
```

roughly becomes hardware like:

```text
                ┌── NOT ──┐
Neg_Flag ───────┤         │
                │         ▼
                │       ┌─────┐
                └──────►│ MUX │──► Neg
                        └──┬──┘
                           │
                  Crnt_Instrn(25)
```

So some of those triangular-looking shapes in your schematic are implementing selections created by the `if/else` statements.

Likewise, your VHDL has:

```vhdl
if (Crnt_Instrn(23 downto 16) = "00000000") then
    Take_Branch <= Neg;
elsif (Crnt_Instrn(23 downto 16) = "00000001") then
    Take_Branch <= Zro;
elsif (Crnt_Instrn(23 downto 16) = "00000010") then
    Take_Branch <= Carry;
elsif (Crnt_Instrn(23 downto 16) = "00111111") then
    Take_Branch <= Jmp;
```

That requires DC to build logic that examines **eight instruction bits**, determines which pattern matches, and selects the appropriate condition. That's why you see a fairly large network of logic on the left/middle.

Then further to the right, this section:

```vhdl
case CurrentState is
    when WRITEBACK =>

        if (Crnt_Instrn(31 downto 30) = "00") then

            if ((Crnt_Instrn(29) = '1' or
                 Crnt_Instrn(28) = '1') and
                 Take_Branch = '1') then

                Brnch_Addr <= '1';

            end if;

            if (Crnt_Instrn(27) = '1') then
                Rtn_Addr <= '1';
            end if;

        end if;
```

gets translated into more **decode + AND/OR + mux logic**.

And finally:

```vhdl
if (Rtn_Addr ='1' or Brnch_Addr = '1') then
    Incrmnt_PC <= '0';
else
    Incrmnt_PC <= '1';
end if;
```

produces the logic leading toward your three outputs:

```text
Incrmnt_PC
Ld_Brnch_Addr
Ld_Rtn_Addr
```

So conceptually your entire screenshot is doing this:

```text
             Crnt_Instrn
                  │
                  ▼
        ┌───────────────────┐
Flags → │ condition decoding │
        └─────────┬─────────┘
                  │
             Take_Branch
                  │
                  ▼
        ┌───────────────────┐
State → │ WRITEBACK decoding │
        │ + instruction bits │
        └─────────┬─────────┘
                  │
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       ▼          ▼          ▼
  Incrmnt_PC  Ld_Brnch   Ld_Rtn
```

### Why isn't this the final gate-level netlist yet?

This is the important part.

Right now DC has translated your VHDL into something logically equivalent to hardware, but it hasn't yet said:

> “Use this exact NAND gate, this exact inverter, this exact mux from `sc_max.db`.”

That happens later when we run:

```tcl
compile_ultra -no_autoungroup
```

So right now:

```text
VHDL
 ↓
generic GTECH logic     ← YOU ARE HERE
 ↓
compile_ultra
 ↓
real cells from sc_max.db
```

After compile, if you revisit this schematic, it should look different because the generic logic will have been **optimized and technology-mapped**.

Also, don't worry about trying to understand every gate in this screenshot. That's not really the purpose of this step. The key lesson is:

> **Your relatively compact behavioral RTL has already been elaborated into a network of actual logical hardware structures, but those structures are still generic rather than technology-specific.**

Now you can **right-click → Back** to return to the TOP schematic. Then we're essentially done with the schematic exploration portion and can proceed to **Task 6: constraints**, where `TOP.con` becomes important.
